 { The best traps are in my opinion moves that also have other attributes, and at times, it can be hard to distinguish between traps and pure prophylaxis. If our opponent is threatening something and we prevent it, and yet he goes for it anyway, because he does not understand the significance of our last move, does it count as a trap? Boris Avrukh – Namig Guliyev, Turin (ol) 2006 White has won a piece, but his bishop does not have a good square. For example } ( 12. Bh6 f5 $1 13. Ng3 Nb4 { and the white queen is lost. } ) 
 { But Boris is quite resourceful and finds a prophylactic idea that includes a small trap: } 12. Bh4 $1 { The best move, as well as a trap. } Nb4 $2 { Black gets too greedy and tries to win the queen. Personally, I would think this is rather naïve and if I had been in Black’s position, against an opponent of Avrukh’s calibre, I would have been more suspicious. Or this is at least something I like to tell myself. To avoid being disillusioned, I will not check my games to see whether this is true or not! . } ( 12... Rc8 $132 { was better. } ) 
13. Qxb4 $1 Bxb4 14. Nxf6+ Kf8 15. Rxd4 $16 { White’s attack is easy to underestimate; he has only two pieces for the queen. But it is already close to unstoppable. } Qa5 $6 { This gets blasted off the board. } ( 15... Be7 16. Nxd7 Ke8 { was maybe better. But after } 17. Nf6 Bxf6 18. Rxd8 Bxd8 19. Bg3 { White’s technical advantage is quite significant. } ) 
16. Nxd7+ Ke8 17. Nf6+ Kf8 18. Ne5 $5 ( 18. Bxc4 { here and on the previous move was allegedly stronger. But as White has a winning attack anyway, we skate past this effortlessly. } ) 
h5 19. Bxc4 Kg7 $6 { The best defensive try was } ( 19... Bc3 $1 { when White is close to winning, but after } 20. Ned7 Kg7 21. Rd3 Bxf6 22. Bxf6 Kh6 23. Bc3 $1 { he will face a continuous technical challenge to prove this. } ) 
20. Nfd7 $1 { Black is not going to make it. } Rhc8 21. Kb1 b5 22. Bf6+ Kg8 23. Rh4 $1 Be7 24. Bxe7 Rxc4 25. Nf6+ Kg7 26. Rxh5 
