 { Finally, I want to say just a few words about general resistance. There are times when you will be unquestionably on the run with your opponent shooting everything he’s got at you as if you were the bear running around the track in the shooting tent. At such times, traps can be useful, but please do not despair and try for cheap tricks and traps just because they can come in handy at some point. Even when hope is slim, it is not gone. In such a scenario, it is generally a good idea to look for variations and positions where it is difficult for your opponent to find a win, rather than trying one-move traps. The general notion is that, if it is hard for you to find the decisive blow, your opponent is more likely to struggle as well. While fighting to find ways to prolong your agony, if not downright escape the pain, it is sensible to look for ways for the opponent to make mistakes as well and be prepared with a surprise or two. Baskaran Adhiban – Maxim Turov, Wijk aan Zee 2012 Black is dead lost. However, he found a very nice way of making his opponent’s job far more difficult. The natural move was probably } ( 18... Be8 { but White is winning in several ways. For example: } 19. Nd4 c5 { and now either } 20. d6 { Or } ( 19... Rh8 19. Ndxe6 $1 fxe6 21. d6 Ra7 22. Nxe6 { and Black’s position is clearly crumbling. } ) 
 { So Black has to come up with something that makes White’s job at least a little bit harder. } ) 
g5 $5 ( 18... exd5 $6 19. cxd5 g5 { is a poor version. Besides the computer solution of } 20. e6 $1 { White also has the human win } ( 19... g5 19. Bh3 $5 { when Black’s position collapses: } g4 21. e6 gxh3 22. exf7 Kf8 23. Qc2 Bxd5 24. Qh7 Bxf7 25. Ne5 { with a mating attack. } ) 
 ) 
19. Nh5 $6 { White is relying on a pawn-break in the centre. Turov was hoping for this and had a surprising answer prepared. White was winning after } ( 19. Nd4 $1 gxf4 20. Qg4 Kf8 ( 20. Nh5 Kh8 21. Qxf4 Bg5 22. Qxf7 { and the black position is falling apart. } ) 
21. Qh5 exd5 22. Qxh6 { followed by e5-e6 and Bh3 with a decisive attack on the light squares. Also strong was the less natural } ) 
 ( 19. Nxe6 $5 fxe6 20. Qc2 Be8 21. Nd4 { when it is hard to believe that Black will survive, even though a direct win does not exist. } ) 
exd5 $1 20. e6 $2 ( 20. Nd4 $1 { is the strongest move, and the endgame after } dxc4 21. Bxc6 Nxc6 22. Nxc6 Rxd1 23. Nxe7 Kf8 24. Nxc8 Rxa1 25. Rxa1 Rxc8 { should not hold for Black in the long run. But in the long run, we are all dead; the job of the soldier is to fight to live another day, not to concern himself with what may happen beyond then. } ) 
Qxe6 $1 21. Re1 { Apparently, White was of the understanding that he was about to win here. But Turov had a surprise for him. } ( 21. Nd4 { is no longer strong. Black can play } Qg6 { with a good position. } ) 
dxc4 $3 { The surprise. White was expecting things such as } ( 21... Qg6 22. Ne5 { And } ) 
 ( 21... Qg4 22. Rxe7 Qxh5 23. Qe1 Nd7 24. Nd4 Bb7 25. Ne6 $1 { in both cases with a winning attack. } ) 
 { Basically, White has fallen into a well hidden trap after which the win is no longer obvious. } 22. Qc1 $2 { Stronger was: } ( 22. Qb1 $1 { White would retain an advantage after: } Qd6 23. Ne5 Bxg2 24. Nxf7 $1 { The difficult move to spot perhaps? } Qd3 $1 25. Nxd8 Qxb1 26. Raxb1 Bxd8 27. Kxg2 Nc6 $14 { White has winning chances here, but his task has been made increasingly difficult. In the game, he was already worse. } ) 
Qf5 $1 23. Re5 ( 23. Rxe7 Bxf3 24. Qxc4 Bxg2 25. Kxg2 c5 $17 ) 
Qg6 $2 ( 23... Qd7 $1 { ∓ was stronger. The advantage is now returned to White, but after having blown it once he is already quite insecure in his play and clearly struggling with his time consumption. All in all I am tempted to say that Black won convincingly. } ) 
24. Rxe7 Qxh5 25. Ne5 Bxg2 26. Kxg2 Qe2 27. Nc6 Qd3 28. Nxd8 Qxd8 29. Re4 c3 30. Qd1 $2 ( 30. Rxb4 Qd5 31. f3 Nc6 32. Re4 $14 ) 
Nc6 $17 31. Rc4 Ne5 32. Qxd8+ Rxd8 33. Rc5 f6 34. a3 a5 35. axb4 axb4 36. Rxc7 Rd7 37. Raa7 Rxc7 38. Rxc7 g4 39. h3 Nd3 40. Kf1 gxh3 41. Kg1 Ne1 42. Kh2 c2 43. Kxh3 b3 { The ability to find ways for the opponent to go wrong is as important as any other way to win in chess. I hope working through the exercises here will help you in improving this ability. If not, then count yourself lucky that there will not be any traps reappearing in the tests later in the book! } 
 0-1

